course: dictionary
lesson: 4561
title: Heck: a mild word with many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Heck» is a mild word. People say it when they are annoyed or surprised, as a softer stand-in for «hell», which some people think is too strong to say. «"What the heck?" said Mum when she saw the muddy footprints.»
explain: After «a», heck can be a noun meaning a lot. «It was a heck of a long walk.» means it was a very long walk. The words «a heck of a» are a way of adding emphasis, like saying «really» or «very much».
explain: «What the heck» can also mean why not, I don't care. «"We might as well try it, what the heck!"» Look at the words around heck to find out which meaning the writer chose: is the speaker annoyed, emphasising something, or shrugging?
fact: «Heck» is a mild word, a softer version of «hell».
fact: «Heck!» is an exclamation: people say it when they are annoyed or surprised.
fact: «A heck of a» means very much: «a heck of a lot» means a great deal.
fact: «What the heck» can mean why not, I don't care.
fact: Heck is an everyday word in all parts of the English-speaking world, especially the US and UK, and is used by children, teachers and parents.
exercise: «Heck!» said Sam when he tripped over his bag. Why did Sam say heck? | *To show he was annoyed or surprised | To count his bags | To say he was very happy | To ask for a drink
exercise: «It was a heck of a long walk.» What does «a heck of a» mean here? | Not at all | *Very much | A kind of bird | A tiny bit
exercise: «We might as well go, what the heck.» What does «what the heck» mean here? | Where is the heck? | *Why not, I don't care | Stop right now | Please be quiet
test: Why is heck a good word for children to use? | *Because it is a mild word, a soft stand-in for hell | Because it is a number | Because it means hell | Because it is a rude word that is allowed
test: «It cost a heck of a lot of money.» What does the phrase mean? | *A great deal of money | A tiny amount of money | No money at all | Money for a heck
exam: Which sentence uses heck to mean why not, I don't care? | "It was a heck of a day." | "The heck was in the box." | "Heck, I dropped my pencil." | *"Let's go, what the heck!"
